ame":"Appraisal Clause Representation","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/party-appraiser-representation/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Homeowner Claim Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/homeowner-dispute-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Commercial, Condo & HOA Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/commercial-condo-association-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Roof & Hail Claim Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/roof-hail-loss-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Windstorm & Microburst Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/windstorm-microburst-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Nor’easter & Hurricane Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/noreaster-hurricane-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Water Damage Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/water-damage-loss-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Fire, Smoke & Puffback Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/fire-smoke-puffback-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Ice Dam & Snow Load Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/ice-dam-snow-load-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Fallen Tree Damage Appraisal","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/fallen-tree-limb-appraisal/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Insurance Estimate Review","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/carrier-estimate-line-review/"}},{"@type":"Offer","itemOffered":{"@type":"Service","name":"Umpire & Neutral Services","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/umpire-neutral-panel-services/"}}]}},{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/#website","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/","name":"Maine Insurance Appraiser","publisher":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#webpage","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/","name":"How Insurance Appraisal Works in Maine | 7 Stages","description":"A step-by-step guide to insurance appraisal in Maine: the written demand, naming appraisers, choosing an umpire, reconciling and the award.","inLanguage":"en-US","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/#website"},"about":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"dateModified":"2026-11-12","primaryImageOfPage":{"@type":"ImageObject","@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#primaryimage","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp","width":1200,"height":800},"mainEntity":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#howto"},"breadcrumb":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#breadcrumb"}},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The appraisal path","item":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/"}],"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#breadcrumb"},{"@type":"HowTo","name":"How insurance appraisal works for a Maine property claim","totalTime":"P120D","step":[{"@type":"HowToStep","position":1,"name":"Pin down the disagreement and read the clause","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-1","text":"Appraisal only works when the fight is about money, not about whether the loss is covered. Confirm that the carrier has accepted the claim in writing and paid, or offered to pay, something. A denial, a reservation of rights or a dispute over what caused the damage is a different problem that a panel generally cannot resolve. Then put the gap on paper and open your policy to the condition titled “Appraisal.” Check these points: Exactly which items, quantities and unit prices separate the two estimates Who may demand appraisal and whether the demand must be in writing How many days each side has to name an appraiser and choose an umpire Who pays the appraisers and the umpire Whether an endorsement changes the base wording or limits what the panel decides Surplus lines and commercial forms sometimes add extra steps. If anything is unclear, or a policy deadline is close, ask a Maine attorney before you send a demand."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":2,"name":"Send a written demand","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-2","text":"Once you understand the clause, put the demand in writing. A short letter or email to the adjuster is usually enough, and it should say plainly that you are invoking the appraisal provision of the policy because the parties disagree on the amount of loss. A solid demand typically includes: Your name, the property address and the claim number The date of loss and the type of damage A sentence identifying the disagreement as one over amount, not coverage The name and contact information of your appraiser, if you have chosen one Send it in a way you can prove, such as certified mail or email with a read receipt, and keep a copy. The date the carrier receives your demand often starts the clock for naming appraisers, so note it in a deadline tracker the same day."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":3,"name":"Each side names its appraiser","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-3","text":"Many policies give each party 20 days after the demand to select a competent and disinterested appraiser and tell the other side who it is. Your appraiser advocates for an accurate number on your behalf but should have no financial stake in the outcome beyond a fee agreed in advance. When choosing an appraiser, look for: Hands-on knowledge of Maine construction, from cedar shingle capes to standing-seam roofs Comfort reading and writing line-item estimates in the software carriers use No prior relationship that could make them look interested, such as being your contractor A written fee arrangement you understand before work begins The carrier will name its own appraiser, often someone who works on carrier-side files. That is normal. The two appraisers are expected to work toward a fair figure, and the umpire stands behind them if they cannot get there."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":4,"name":"The appraisers agree on an umpire","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-4","text":"After both appraisers are named, they choose a neutral umpire together. The umpire only becomes involved on items the two appraisers cannot settle, but having one in place early keeps the process moving and discourages stalling. Umpires are often experienced estimators, contractors, engineers or retired adjusters. A good candidate is: Independent of both appraisers and both parties Familiar with the type of loss, whether that is ice dam damage, a puffback or a commercial roof Available within a reasonable time, which matters after a big storm If the appraisers cannot agree on an umpire within the time the policy sets, often 15 days, many policies allow either party to ask a court of record in the state where the property sits to appoint one. In Maine that typically means a Superior Court petition, which is a step to take with an attorney’s guidance."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":5,"name":"Inspect, exchange estimates and reconcile","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-5","text":"This is where most of the work happens. The two appraisers inspect the property, ideally together, and measure what needs measuring: roof squares, wall areas, flooring, trim and contents if they are part of the dispute. Each appraiser then prepares or refines an estimate and shares it with the other. Reconciliation goes line by line. For each item, the appraisers ask: Is this repair actually needed because of the covered loss? Is the quantity right? Is the unit price realistic for this town and this season? Does matching, code work or access cost belong here? Items they agree on are locked in. Many files settle at this stage without the umpire, because two knowledgeable people working from the same measurements usually close most of the gap. Disputed items go on a short list for the umpire, with each side’s figure and reasoning attached."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":6,"name":"The umpire resolves what remains","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-6","text":"The umpire reviews only the items the appraisers could not agree on. That may involve reading both estimates, looking at photos and reports, and sometimes visiting the property. The umpire then picks a figure for each disputed item, which may land on either side’s number or somewhere between. The result is written into an appraisal award, usually a form listing the amount of loss and, where the policy calls for it, replacement cost and actual cash value figures. Under typical wording, an award signed by any two of the three panel members is binding on the amount of loss. The appraisers sign if they agree on every item One appraiser and the umpire sign if the umpire breaks a deadlock Each line on the award should be clear enough for the carrier to process payment"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":7,"name":"Payment and the limits of an award","url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#step-7","text":"After the award is signed, the carrier calculates payment by applying the deductible, any prior payments and, if the policy pays actual cash value first, the depreciation holdback that you recover once repairs are done. Most policies require payment within a set number of days after the award, and Maine law separately addresses timely claim payment. It helps to know what appraisal does not decide: Whether the loss is covered at all Whether the carrier acted in bad faith, which is a legal claim Policy interpretation questions, such as whether an exclusion applies Causation is a gray area. Some courts and policies let the panel decide how much damage a covered event caused, while others treat that as a coverage question. If the carrier pushes back on an award or raises coverage after it, talk to a Maine attorney about your options."}],"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/the-appraisal-path/#howto"}]}</script>
